=== accelerator-work --help ===
The `accelerator-work` command-line surface: work-item lifecycle primitives (`create`, `show`, `resolve`, `diff`, `update`) plus small utility subcommands used by the skills that orchestrate them

Usage: accelerator-work <COMMAND>

Commands:
  resolve           Resolve a user-supplied work-item identifier (a path, a full ID, or a bare number) to a canonical file path
  template-hints    Print the work-item template's hint values for a frontmatter field (e.g. `kind`, `status`, `priority`), one per line. Always exits 0
  show              Print a work item: the whole file, or a single frontmatter field
  diff              Show a per-section diff between two work-item-shaped files, for conflict-resolution review. `local` is the `-` side, `remote` is the `+` side
  create            Atomically create a new work item under the configured pattern, self-allocating its own ID
  update            Atomically apply field/tag/list-field edits to an existing work item's frontmatter
  link-external-id  Write an already-created remote id into a local draft's `external_id` frontmatter field (upsert)
  canonicalise-id   Canonicalise a work-item ID under the configured pattern (zero-pads a bare number, prepends the default project when the pattern needs one)
  next-number       Print the next N sequential IDs the configured pattern would allocate. Display-only: never writes a file or commits a number
  list              List work items from the configured directory, filtered and rendered as a table — or as a parent/child tree with `--hierarchy`. Carries a sync-status column when `work.integration` names a tracker. Read-only: never writes a file and never contacts the remote to mutate it
  sync              Reconcile local work items with the configured remote tracker (`work.integration`)
  help              Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help  Print help

=== accelerator-work resolve --help ===
Resolve a user-supplied work-item identifier (a path, a full ID, or a bare number) to a canonical file path

Usage: accelerator-work resolve <INPUT>

Arguments:
  <INPUT>  The identifier to resolve: a path, a full ID (e.g. `PROJ-0042` or `0042`), or a bare legacy number (e.g. `42`)

Options:
  -h, --help  Print help

=== accelerator-work template-hints --help ===
Print the work-item template's hint values for a frontmatter field (e.g. `kind`, `status`, `priority`), one per line. Always exits 0

Usage: accelerator-work template-hints <FIELD>

Arguments:
  <FIELD>  The frontmatter field to extract hints for

Options:
  -h, --help  Print help

=== accelerator-work show --help ===
Print a work item: the whole file, or a single frontmatter field

Usage: accelerator-work show [OPTIONS] <PATH>

Arguments:
  <PATH>  The work-item file to read

Options:
      --field <FIELD>  Print only this frontmatter field's raw value instead of the whole file
  -h, --help           Print help

=== accelerator-work diff --help ===
Show a per-section diff between two work-item-shaped files, for conflict-resolution review. `local` is the `-` side, `remote` is the `+` side

Usage: accelerator-work diff <LOCAL> <REMOTE>

Arguments:
  <LOCAL>   The baseline (`-`) file
  <REMOTE>  The changed (`+`) file

Options:
  -h, --help  Print help

=== accelerator-work create --help ===
Atomically create a new work item under the configured pattern, self-allocating its own ID

Usage: accelerator-work create [OPTIONS] <TITLE> <KIND> <PRIORITY>

Arguments:
  <TITLE>     The title (a short noun phrase)
  <KIND>      The kind (e.g. `story`, `epic`, `task`, `bug`, `spike`)
  <PRIORITY>  The priority (e.g. `high`, `medium`, `low`)

Options:
      --status <STATUS>              The initial status [default: draft]
      --parent <PARENT>              The parent work item reference (`work-item:NNNN`)
      --tag <TAGS>                   A tag to add; repeatable
      --block <BLOCKS>               A work item this one blocks; repeatable
      --blocked-by <BLOCKED_BY>      A work item that blocks this one; repeatable
      --derived-from <DERIVED_FROM>  A document this item was derived from; repeatable
      --relates-to <RELATES_TO>      A related work item; repeatable
      --source <SOURCE>              The source document reference
      --project <PROJECT>            The project code, when the configured pattern needs one
      --author <AUTHOR>              The author. Falls back to the current VCS identity when omitted
      --producer <PRODUCER>          The producer name recorded in the frontmatter [default: accelerator-work]
      --body-file <BODY_FILE>        A file whose content becomes the body (with `NNNN` and the title placeholder substituted), instead of the template's own skeleton body
      --push                         Push the new item to the configured remote tracker before writing it locally. On a retryable failure the create is retried once, then the item is saved unsynced; a terminal failure is reported and the item saved unsynced (a remote issue may already exist — see `exit_codes` for the 70/71 contract). The file is written either way
      --dry-run                      Preview the fields a `--push` create would resolve against the configured tracker, then exit without writing a file or creating a remote issue. Prints one tab-separated line the create skill parses; exits 70 when the tracker is unreachable (the preview could not be resolved), so the caller can distinguish an unreachable tracker from an unresolvable configured value (reported as an `unresolvable` source on the line, exit 0)
  -h, --help                         Print help

=== accelerator-work update --help ===
Atomically apply field/tag/list-field edits to an existing work item's frontmatter

Usage: accelerator-work update [OPTIONS] <PATH>

Arguments:
  <PATH>  The work-item file to update

Options:
      --set <SETS>                A `KEY=VALUE` scalar field to set; repeatable. Rejects `id`/ `work_item_id` and every list-typed field (use `--append`/ `--remove`/`--add-tag`/`--remove-tag` for those instead)
      --add-tag <ADD_TAGS>        A tag to add; repeatable
      --remove-tag <REMOVE_TAGS>  A tag to remove; repeatable
      --append <APPENDS>          A `KEY=VALUE` pair appending `VALUE` to list-typed field `KEY` (`blocks`, `blocked_by`, `derived_from`, `relates_to`); repeatable
      --remove <REMOVES>          A `KEY=VALUE` pair removing `VALUE` from list-typed field `KEY`; repeatable
      --push                      Replace the item's remote issue with its whole rendered content before writing the edit locally. Refused when the item has no `external_id` — see `create --push` for an unsynced item. Unlike `create --push`, a failed push leaves the local file untouched: exit 70 (retryable, unchanged) or 71 (terminal, baseline entry cleared so the next `sync` reconciles it as a conflict)
  -h, --help                      Print help

=== accelerator-work link-external-id --help ===
Write an already-created remote id into a local draft's `external_id` frontmatter field (upsert)

Usage: accelerator-work link-external-id <PATH> <EXTERNAL_ID>

Arguments:
  <PATH>         The work-item file to link
  <EXTERNAL_ID>  The remote identifier to record

Options:
  -h, --help  Print help

=== accelerator-work canonicalise-id --help ===
Canonicalise a work-item ID under the configured pattern (zero-pads a bare number, prepends the default project when the pattern needs one)

Usage: accelerator-work canonicalise-id <INPUT>

Arguments:
  <INPUT>  The ID or bare number to canonicalise

Options:
  -h, --help  Print help

=== accelerator-work next-number --help ===
Print the next N sequential IDs the configured pattern would allocate. Display-only: never writes a file or commits a number

Usage: accelerator-work next-number [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --project <PROJECT>  The project code, when the configured pattern needs one
      --count <COUNT>      How many sequential IDs to print [default: 1]
  -h, --help               Print help

=== accelerator-work list --help ===
List work items from the configured directory, filtered and rendered as a table — or as a parent/child tree with `--hierarchy`. Carries a sync-status column when `work.integration` names a tracker. Read-only: never writes a file and never contacts the remote to mutate it

Usage: accelerator-work list [OPTIONS] [TERM]

Arguments:
  [TERM]  Keep only items whose title contains this substring (case-insensitive)

Options:
      --status <STATUS>      Keep only items whose `status` equals this value exactly
      --kind <KIND>          Keep only items whose `kind` equals this value exactly
      --priority <PRIORITY>  Keep only items whose `priority` equals this value exactly
      --parent <PARENT>      Keep only items whose `parent` canonicalises to this value (short and long ID forms compare equal)
      --tag <TAGS>           Keep only items carrying this tag; repeatable, and every one supplied must be present
      --hierarchy            Render the parent/child hierarchy as a tree instead of a table
  -h, --help                 Print help

=== accelerator-work sync --help ===
Reconcile local work items with the configured remote tracker (`work.integration`).

A run may author **new** artefacts on both sides beyond updating existing ones: it creates remote issues from unsynced local drafts (bounded by `--max-pushes`, counted as pushes) and pulls untracked remote issues into new local files (bounded by `--max-pulls`, counted as pulls). Untracked discovery is scoped to the configured project, and a run that would exceed either bound refuses with zero writes (exit 5).

Exit codes: 0 clean; 4 items await a human (unresolved conflicts, skipped-dirty pulls, remote-absent or indeterminate items); 70 a read failed or every per-item failure was retryable; 71 any per-item failure was terminal; 1 an internal error; 2 a usage error; 5 refused (would exceed --max-pulls/--max-pushes, zero writes); 72 the configured tracker is recognised but has no client built yet; 73 work.integration is unset or names an unrecognised tracker; 74 the configured tracker is wired but its configuration or credentials are missing (nothing was sent). The report on stdout is authoritative: check it for `unresolved` lines regardless of exit code, since a 71 run may also carry conflicts.

Usage: accelerator-work sync [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --push-only
          Push local changes only; never write a local file

      --pull-only
          Pull remote changes only; never write to the remote

      --preview
          Report the actions a run would take without touching any work item. Remote reads still occur (this is what counts against rate limits) and gitignored conflict dossiers are still written, but no create, update or work-item write does

      --resolve <RESOLUTIONS>
          An `<id>=<remote|local|skip>` order for a reported conflict; repeatable. An unrecognised token is treated as `skip`, with a warning naming the token and the accepted set

      --per-item-reads
          Read each item with its own request instead of one bulk retrieval

      --max-pulls <MAX_PULLS>
          Refuse the run if it would overwrite more than this many local files from the remote. 0 refuses every pull
          
          [default: 25]

      --max-pushes <MAX_PUSHES>
          Refuse the run if it would replace more than this many remote issues. 0 refuses every push
          
          [default: 25]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

